#
# A command substitution whose body will not parse, written inside a subshell
# (#3274).
#
# A file of its own, and the discriminating case is last, because in five of
# the seven columns it ends the script: anything written after it would be
# graded in the columns that carry on and never reached in the columns that do
# not, which is a file that asks two different questions of two halves of the
# panel.
#
# BusyBox ash 1.37.0 writes `start` and exits 2, exactly as dash does. Graded
# in the container against the BusyBox in it, which is the only place this
# column has a reference at all.
#
# The controls come first for the same reason they are here at all. The same
# substitution at the top level is fatal in every column and was already right;
# a plain parse error inside a subshell is a *file* that does not parse, so it
# cannot be written here at all. What is left is the pair — a substitution's
# failure, inside a subshell — and that is the last block.

printf 'the controls first\n'

# The word expands to nothing and the command that held it runs, which says
# the body was read at all.
v=$(echo hi); printf 'an ordinary body -> [%s]\n' "$v"

# A subshell that ends on its own is not this: the script carries on past it
# in every column.
( exit 7 ); printf 'a subshell that exits -> %s\n' "$?"

# And a failing command inside a substitution inside a subshell is not this
# either: the body ran, it just did not succeed.
( w=$(false; echo out); printf 'a failing body -> [%s]\n' "$w" )
printf 'after a failing body -> %s\n' "$?"

# The discriminating case, and the last thing in this file.
printf 'start\n'
( v=$(echo hi; for); printf 'inner carried on\n' )
printf 'after the subshell st=%s\n' "$?"
